1
00:00:03,148 --> 00:00:08,553
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been coming across a sutta a few days ago.

2
00:00:09,914 --> 00:00:13,677
[SPEAKER_00]: The question is, is suffering created by oneself?

3
00:00:13,697 --> 00:00:16,139
[SPEAKER_00]: Is suffering created by another?

4
00:00:16,179 --> 00:00:19,962
[SPEAKER_00]: Is suffering created by both oneself or another?

5
00:00:20,563 --> 00:00:26,668
[SPEAKER_00]: Or is suffering created by neither, like just arises randomly?

6
00:00:26,708 --> 00:00:30,711
[SPEAKER_00]: So I was contemplating, like, the Buddha said, like, it's neither of those, basically.

7
00:00:31,988 --> 00:00:35,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Yet, we often say, inside the hermitage, that you are responsible.

8
00:00:37,471 --> 00:00:47,715
[SPEAKER_00]: And so, I think it would be good to, like, kind of distinguish what's the difference between being responsible and you are creator of your suffering.

9
00:00:47,795 --> 00:00:51,617
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there is a kind of a difference there, but I think it would be good to... Yeah.

10
00:00:53,938 --> 00:00:57,740
[SPEAKER_04]: What's the difference between being responsible for your suffering and creating your suffering?

11
00:01:01,574 --> 00:01:20,466
[SPEAKER_04]: Because there is that common view in some teachings where you would be told that, kind of, in the moment, you create the suffering by, kind of, the way you direct your attention or something like that.

12
00:01:20,646 --> 00:01:27,471
[SPEAKER_04]: So, is that the same as saying that you're responsible for your suffering?

13
00:01:27,511 --> 00:01:29,092
[SPEAKER_04]: Is that what you mean when you say that?

14
00:01:41,085 --> 00:01:49,432
[SPEAKER_02]: So, maybe we should start clarifying what the suffering is, basically.

15
00:01:50,293 --> 00:01:54,136
[SPEAKER_02]: Because I think this is the most important question.

16
00:01:54,477 --> 00:01:54,717
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

17
00:01:57,139 --> 00:01:59,361
[SPEAKER_02]: Go on.

18
00:02:04,345 --> 00:02:06,147
[SPEAKER_02]: I can say the suffering,

19
00:02:13,288 --> 00:02:14,469
[SPEAKER_04]: Right, that's the root of the suffering.

20
00:02:14,549 --> 00:02:15,990
[SPEAKER_04]: What is the suffering itself?

21
00:02:20,313 --> 00:02:25,357
[SPEAKER_02]: Not seeing the mind that is untamed.

22
00:02:28,459 --> 00:02:36,444
[SPEAKER_04]: It's basically the same as... Yeah, that's still the root or the cause of the suffering.

23
00:02:36,464 --> 00:02:38,906
[SPEAKER_04]: Like you say, ignorance is a cause of suffering.

24
00:02:39,447 --> 00:02:41,448
[SPEAKER_04]: But the suffering itself, let's say, like...

25
00:02:42,726 --> 00:02:47,547
[SPEAKER_04]: Phenomenologically, without looking at something else, you just experience suffering right now, let's say.

26
00:02:48,947 --> 00:02:50,568
[SPEAKER_04]: Just, what is that suffering?

27
00:02:55,089 --> 00:02:56,029
[SPEAKER_04]: Without overthinking it.

28
00:03:02,730 --> 00:03:03,490
[SPEAKER_00]: It's unpleasant.

29
00:03:03,711 --> 00:03:04,511
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

30
00:03:04,571 --> 00:03:05,811
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just an unpleasant feeling.

31
00:03:07,191 --> 00:03:10,032
[SPEAKER_04]: So, yes, you can think about why the suffering is there,

32
00:03:11,448 --> 00:03:13,830
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's pretty much what we're going to go into.

33
00:03:13,870 --> 00:03:18,853
[SPEAKER_04]: But first of all, it's important to be clear that, yeah, that's what suffering is.

34
00:03:19,153 --> 00:03:22,816
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just experiencing unpleasant, unpleasantness.

35
00:03:23,496 --> 00:03:23,936
[SPEAKER_01]: But wait.

36
00:03:24,897 --> 00:03:26,118
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that the origin of the suffering?

37
00:03:27,420 --> 00:03:27,820
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay.

38
00:03:28,040 --> 00:03:29,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that the origin of the suffering?

39
00:03:29,281 --> 00:03:30,041
[SPEAKER_04]: No, no, no, no.

40
00:03:30,221 --> 00:03:30,761
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what we said.

41
00:03:31,081 --> 00:03:34,082
[SPEAKER_04]: We're gonna get into what the origin of the suffering is.

42
00:03:34,482 --> 00:03:37,763
[SPEAKER_04]: But now we're just saying, suffering in of itself, what is it?

43
00:03:38,623 --> 00:03:39,683
[SPEAKER_04]: Dukkha.

44
00:03:39,804 --> 00:03:41,284
[SPEAKER_04]: Just dukkha, vedanā.

45
00:03:41,304 --> 00:03:42,984
[SPEAKER_04]: Dukkha, feeling, unpleasant feeling.

46
00:03:43,064 --> 00:03:43,345
[SPEAKER_04]: Okay.

47
00:03:43,825 --> 00:03:44,525
[SPEAKER_04]: So, eh...

48
00:03:46,033 --> 00:03:47,133
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's what it is.

49
00:03:47,393 --> 00:03:48,534
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's what we're talking about.

50
00:03:48,554 --> 00:04:00,398
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not some... because people can get into kind of metaphysical, almost like, proliferations about suffering, being this and being that.

51
00:04:00,798 --> 00:04:06,820
[SPEAKER_04]: But really, it's just right here and now, you feel an unpleasant feeling.

52
00:04:07,020 --> 00:04:07,700
[SPEAKER_04]: There's some kind of

53
00:04:09,152 --> 00:04:15,417
[SPEAKER_04]: Dissonance, conflict, dissatisfaction, unhappiness.

54
00:04:16,157 --> 00:04:16,697
[SPEAKER_04]: Resistance?

55
00:04:17,638 --> 00:04:21,381
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, that would be what's causing the suffering.

56
00:04:22,121 --> 00:04:23,683
[SPEAKER_04]: But the suffering itself?

57
00:04:23,763 --> 00:04:24,023
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

58
00:04:24,143 --> 00:04:25,944
[SPEAKER_04]: It's just... yeah.

59
00:04:28,486 --> 00:04:30,007
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's what the suffering is.

60
00:04:30,107 --> 00:04:36,732
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, let's say you're experiencing an unpleasant feeling, on account of whatever.

61
00:04:38,370 --> 00:04:47,497
[SPEAKER_04]: Can you get rid of it immediately?

62
00:04:47,817 --> 00:05:01,548
[SPEAKER_04]: So, can you even kind of be sure that things that you will do will result in that feeling going away, at any point?

63
00:05:05,211 --> 00:05:05,971
[SPEAKER_03]: Okay, sure.

64
00:05:06,912 --> 00:05:29,619
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you can hope that, you know, traveling the world and attaining all the riches in the world and getting all you want, even if you actually get what you wanted, you can hope that that will result in changing the feeling that has arisen, but it may well not happen.

65
00:05:30,547 --> 00:05:33,589
[SPEAKER_04]: And that's the root of all problems in the world.

66
00:05:34,109 --> 00:05:36,691
[SPEAKER_04]: It's that you have a feeling and you can't change it.

67
00:05:38,952 --> 00:05:48,258
[SPEAKER_04]: So, from that point of view, it becomes quite clear that saying that you are the creator of your suffering would not be quite accurate.

68
00:05:48,598 --> 00:05:53,942
[SPEAKER_04]: Because, if you were, you would be able to decide.

69
00:05:54,422 --> 00:05:55,803
[SPEAKER_04]: And for that matter, actually,

70
00:05:56,973 --> 00:06:17,210
[SPEAKER_04]: If you have tamed your mind and you have undone these origins of suffering that we're going to get into, then even if you said, I want to suffer, I try to suffer, then you wouldn't be able to, which also adds to the point that, no, it's not your creation.

71
00:06:18,515 --> 00:06:20,957
[SPEAKER_04]: Otherwise, it would be fully under your will.

72
00:06:22,377 --> 00:06:26,580
[SPEAKER_00]: So now... Yeah, it wouldn't be a problem in the first place.

73
00:06:27,020 --> 00:06:30,922
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, exactly, because then you could decide, like... Actually, people kind of say that sometimes.

74
00:06:30,942 --> 00:06:37,747
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, they say suffering is a necessary part of life, because it helps you grow.

75
00:06:38,687 --> 00:06:40,848
[SPEAKER_04]: Which isn't completely wrong.

76
00:06:41,048 --> 00:06:43,150
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, it's not like you're trying to to be kind of

77
00:06:44,743 --> 00:06:45,664
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not a problem.

78
00:06:45,684 --> 00:06:46,165
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, it's fine.

79
00:07:04,359 --> 00:07:09,323
[SPEAKER_04]: Kind of implies that, still, that ignorant view that you fundamentally have control over it.

80
00:07:09,844 --> 00:07:21,133
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't really realize that you're pretty much confined within it and that, like, most of the time, the things you do work in changing it.

81
00:07:21,394 --> 00:07:22,995
[SPEAKER_04]: But if you just

82
00:07:24,494 --> 00:07:25,094
[SPEAKER_04]: Contemplate.

83
00:07:25,434 --> 00:07:27,715
[SPEAKER_04]: That's what the Buddha encouraged people.

84
00:07:28,035 --> 00:07:29,335
[SPEAKER_04]: Every day, contemplate.

85
00:07:29,435 --> 00:07:30,535
[SPEAKER_04]: I am liable to sickness.

86
00:07:30,635 --> 00:07:31,816
[SPEAKER_04]: I am liable to aging.

87
00:07:31,896 --> 00:07:32,776
[SPEAKER_04]: I am liable to death.

88
00:07:33,376 --> 00:07:39,257
[SPEAKER_04]: Because those are the things where an unpleasant feeling will arise, and no matter what you do, you won't be able to get rid of it.

89
00:07:40,498 --> 00:07:42,338
[SPEAKER_04]: So...

90
00:07:43,358 --> 00:07:44,339
[SPEAKER_04]: Unless your mind is tamed.

91
00:07:45,099 --> 00:07:51,240
[SPEAKER_04]: So... Then, you recognize... Yeah, so, it is a problem.

92
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:53,681
[SPEAKER_04]: It is something that...

93
00:07:56,982 --> 00:08:05,984
[SPEAKER_04]: I can't kind of cover it up, because factually all of my issues, all of my concerns, revolve around unpleasant cleaning.

94
00:08:07,404 --> 00:08:21,527
[SPEAKER_04]: So now, having established that, what does it mean to say that... because see, it could sound now as if... okay, so it's just you're subjected to it.

95
00:08:22,608 --> 00:08:26,271
[SPEAKER_04]: You were, you're kind of a victim of feeling.

96
00:08:27,552 --> 00:08:31,535
[SPEAKER_04]: And I think that would almost sound as if it's randomly arisen.

97
00:08:32,236 --> 00:08:33,537
[SPEAKER_04]: Like it just comes on its own.

98
00:08:34,338 --> 00:08:40,342
[SPEAKER_04]: Or it just imposed on you by somebody else, by God or by something that you have no control over.

99
00:08:41,323 --> 00:08:43,585
[SPEAKER_04]: So, but we're saying that's not the case.

100
00:08:46,007 --> 00:08:49,210
[SPEAKER_04]: So, what does it mean then to say that you're responsible?

101
00:08:51,792 --> 00:08:52,212
[SPEAKER_04]: Practically.

102
00:08:55,220 --> 00:09:01,506
[SPEAKER_04]: How would you know that, okay, I'm responsible for this feeling that has arisen?

103
00:09:01,826 --> 00:09:12,817
[SPEAKER_04]: If it's not like a momentary thing that I'm just like, kind of, like we said, creating it in the immediate sense, what would it be then?

104
00:09:36,666 --> 00:09:45,814
[SPEAKER_03]: On a practical level, there are choices that I can make that seem to reduce my suffering.

105
00:09:46,554 --> 00:09:49,957
[SPEAKER_03]: So it seems that I do have an influence.

106
00:09:53,139 --> 00:09:54,741
[SPEAKER_04]: But is it a direct influence?

107
00:10:09,289 --> 00:10:10,150
[SPEAKER_03]: I can't say for sure.

108
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:11,351
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

109
00:10:11,851 --> 00:10:12,612
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it depends.

110
00:10:12,892 --> 00:10:13,112
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

111
00:10:14,713 --> 00:10:20,378
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, direct influence would be if you've trained your mind, as we were saying.

112
00:10:21,138 --> 00:10:32,027
[SPEAKER_04]: But, before that, practically speaking, yeah, what you're... well, let me not give you the answer.

113
00:10:32,047 --> 00:10:34,969
[SPEAKER_04]: So, practically speaking, let's say, what would lead

114
00:10:35,499 --> 00:10:35,739
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

115
00:10:35,919 --> 00:10:37,780
[SPEAKER_04]: To the arising of an unpleasant feeling.

116
00:10:37,940 --> 00:10:42,423
[SPEAKER_04]: Not how you could get rid of it, but, like, just how did it come about in the first place.

117
00:10:45,785 --> 00:10:47,806
[SPEAKER_00]: It's because one delights in what's pleasant.

118
00:10:48,047 --> 00:10:56,832
[SPEAKER_00]: Then, when this pleasure disappears, it kind of... like, this dependency on pleasure, like, is felt unpleasantly because now

119
00:10:58,655 --> 00:11:00,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Pleasure is not available anymore.

120
00:11:00,196 --> 00:11:09,363
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so basically, is it correct to say that the unpleasant feeling is just the unpleasant circumstance?

121
00:11:10,483 --> 00:11:15,627
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's more like the... kind of... what I build up in regard to it.

122
00:11:16,548 --> 00:11:25,814
[SPEAKER_00]: What kind of... Because I'm so dependent on pleasure, when this pleasure disappears, I feel like what's left

123
00:11:30,248 --> 00:11:54,347
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, so, in other words, you can have unpleasant circumstances, which are, yeah, like I said, sickness, aging, death, but whether there's an unpleasant feeling felt on account of that, like dissatisfaction or that acute level of the mind being unhappy,

124
00:11:55,550 --> 00:12:05,315
[SPEAKER_04]: That depends not on the situation, depends on what you have cultivated, where your mind is.

125
00:12:06,216 --> 00:12:18,042
[SPEAKER_04]: So, there are certain actions that you would do that would make you be hit or struck by the arrow of pain

126
00:12:19,205 --> 00:12:24,187
[SPEAKER_04]: When unpleasant circumstances arise, which inevitably is going to happen.

127
00:12:24,947 --> 00:12:33,289
[SPEAKER_04]: So, that's why the Buddha said, I think once that he said, there are contacts that are suitable for the arising of unpleasant feeling.

128
00:12:34,290 --> 00:12:41,352
[SPEAKER_04]: And so, when there is the kind of suitable basis, he kind of gave the simile of like

129
00:12:43,623 --> 00:12:48,465
[SPEAKER_04]: Like a... like two blocks of wood coming together or something like that, that they make a noise.

130
00:12:49,225 --> 00:12:57,848
[SPEAKER_04]: It's like, eh... when the suitable conditions are there, this unpleasant contact will arouse an unpleasant feeling.

131
00:12:59,449 --> 00:13:09,312
[SPEAKER_04]: So, it's not talking about some kind of external, eh... relationship of the biochemistry kind of thing.

132
00:13:09,332 --> 00:13:11,053
[SPEAKER_04]: No, it's... it's that internal

133
00:13:13,081 --> 00:13:30,066
[SPEAKER_04]: Like you said, eh... situation or internal, eh... how do you put it... climate of your own mind that you build up based on your actions, that's going to determine whether unpleasant feelings arise in the first place.

134
00:13:30,326 --> 00:13:40,869
[SPEAKER_04]: If you were to actually cultivate actions that don't... that are not suitable for the arising of suffering, eh...

135
00:13:41,322 --> 00:13:51,050
[SPEAKER_04]: Even things that aroused displeasure before, would not... either not perturb the mind, or even lead to the happiness of renunciation.

136
00:13:52,070 --> 00:14:08,303
[SPEAKER_04]: As the sutra would describe, like, when the mind develops jhāna, then rough roads, rough food, or no food, living under a tree, and just not... little to no medicine,

137
00:14:08,960 --> 00:14:14,082
[SPEAKER_04]: would would feel factually like heavenly requisites.

138
00:14:15,303 --> 00:14:28,629
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you realize it's the exact same thing that would have before given rise to displeasure, because of the choices, the right choices that you've been cultivating, choices that were not suitable for the accumulation of craving.

139
00:14:28,809 --> 00:14:33,071
[SPEAKER_04]: Actually, the deaccum... or diminished your craving.

140
00:14:34,071 --> 00:14:37,233
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, you... there is no arising of suffering.

141
00:14:37,433 --> 00:14:37,673
[SPEAKER_04]: So,

142
00:14:39,187 --> 00:14:43,650
[SPEAKER_04]: You can directly see then, so that's what it means to say that you're responsible.

143
00:14:44,790 --> 00:14:55,156
[SPEAKER_04]: It's not about... Of course, it matters what you do in the moment, because in the moment, that's where your direct input is.

144
00:14:56,497 --> 00:15:01,640
[SPEAKER_04]: But, the arising of suffering is determined by what you've cultivated.

145
00:15:04,542 --> 00:15:04,862
[SPEAKER_04]: So,

146
00:15:07,173 --> 00:15:13,236
[SPEAKER_04]: From that point of view, then, you realize... because, as the Buddha said, okay, so craving is the root of suffering.

147
00:15:16,218 --> 00:15:24,743
[SPEAKER_04]: But, does that mean that you can... it's the same question that I asked before, does that mean that you can just...

148
00:15:24,763 --> 00:15:34,708
[SPEAKER_04]: I've been doing whatever I want, not paying attention to my actions, but I have the direct insight of what craving is, so I just cut it off at the last moment.

149
00:15:40,099 --> 00:15:40,319
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

150
00:15:40,339 --> 00:15:41,060
[SPEAKER_04]: That's not possible.

151
00:15:41,080 --> 00:15:41,280
[SPEAKER_04]: No.

152
00:15:43,603 --> 00:15:45,445
[SPEAKER_04]: So, because, see, it's not you.

153
00:15:46,626 --> 00:15:53,274
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, yes, you can, on top of the mind having craving, you can also act out of craving, which means you're making it worse.

154
00:15:54,215 --> 00:15:55,076
[SPEAKER_04]: But, fundamentally,

155
00:15:55,702 --> 00:16:02,887
[SPEAKER_04]: What the Buddha means when he says craving is the root of suffering, it means a mind affected with craving is the root of suffering.

156
00:16:03,047 --> 00:16:10,553
[SPEAKER_04]: Like the wild animal, as we were describing in those few discussions ago, maybe.

157
00:16:11,633 --> 00:16:15,496
[SPEAKER_04]: It's whether you like it or not, basically.

158
00:16:16,417 --> 00:16:21,040
[SPEAKER_04]: Experiencing certain situations with that type of mind will mean suffering is there.

159
00:16:22,398 --> 00:16:27,704
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, there's a bit of a delay between you, on the one hand, and mind.

160
00:16:28,284 --> 00:16:36,153
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mind can be calm, but if you then start behaving inappropriately, you will stir it up, but that's not immediately noticeable.

161
00:16:36,393 --> 00:16:36,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

162
00:16:36,733 --> 00:16:43,400
[SPEAKER_01]: But, at some point, you will notice, and then you can calm down, but it will take then time again for the mind to calm down.

163
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:44,501
[SPEAKER_04]: And how do you calm down?

164
00:16:45,462 --> 00:16:50,225
[SPEAKER_04]: Well, it's by... first of all, you have to stop doing the things that led you to that.

165
00:16:50,265 --> 00:16:50,505
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah.

166
00:16:50,565 --> 00:16:55,028
[SPEAKER_04]: You don't come down by kind of putting out...

167
00:16:55,168 --> 00:16:59,691
[SPEAKER_04]: I grab a perfect fire extinguisher, but I continue to throw wood on the fire.

168
00:16:59,891 --> 00:17:01,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

169
00:17:01,331 --> 00:17:07,215
[SPEAKER_01]: So then the responsibility, which was the original question, the responsibility is on what you do,

170
00:17:08,015 --> 00:17:18,408
[SPEAKER_01]: But depending on, let's say, how pliable the mind is or on how well it has been tamed and hence listens to you, there will be somewhat of a delay.

171
00:17:18,988 --> 00:17:23,253
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you go in the wrong direction and you agitate that mind, then you will suffer on account of it.

172
00:17:24,034 --> 00:17:26,037
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you keep that distinction,

173
00:17:26,473 --> 00:17:34,275
[SPEAKER_01]: Between you and mind, you can see how there can be responsibility and how it is not in conflict with a mind that suffers.

174
00:17:35,215 --> 00:17:51,039
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole point is, of course, to tame the mind in such a way that it cannot become agitated again on account of you, first of all, being restrained and then the mind understanding that it is better off if it is tamed and trained and then it doesn't want to go back anymore.

175
00:17:51,099 --> 00:17:51,319
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

176
00:17:51,579 --> 00:17:54,680
[SPEAKER_01]: That that will be the, let's say, final result of all this.

177
00:17:54,920 --> 00:17:55,820
[SPEAKER_04]: Yeah, and and

178
00:17:56,440 --> 00:18:04,662
[SPEAKER_04]: That brings us to one point that I was thinking about bringing up, but actually this is a good context to do it in.

179
00:18:05,783 --> 00:18:23,608
[SPEAKER_04]: Because sometimes, there's this idea that you should kind of... that if you, let's say, try to incline towards seclusion, and you don't really interact with people, and you don't really go out into the world, then you're just running away.

180
00:18:24,828 --> 00:18:27,489
[SPEAKER_04]: And you you're not really, kind of, developing yourself.

181
00:18:27,569 --> 00:18:37,214
[SPEAKER_04]: So, you actually have to expose yourself to the temptations in order to build up your ability to, kind of, not be moved by them, let's say.

182
00:18:39,706 --> 00:18:45,028
[SPEAKER_04]: So, in light of what we've been discussing now, what's wrong with that?

183
00:18:46,129 --> 00:18:54,493
