00:50:23,284 --> 00:50:40,440
[SPEAKER_04]: Now, you will be, if you develop this principle rightly, you will be even more disinclined to break the precepts, to do anything rooted in redivision and delusion, because all of those things are precisely based on losing this perspective.

541
00:50:40,740 --> 00:50:47,827
[SPEAKER_04]: Losing this perspective of, basically, the the four satipaṭṭhāna, the background phenomena of your experience.

542
00:50:49,164 --> 00:50:57,090
[SPEAKER_04]: Greed, aversion and delusion is that absorption into concerns and not recognizing the whole picture.

543
00:50:58,291 --> 00:51:05,036
[SPEAKER_04]: And the reason why I brought that up partly is because I'm pretty sure people wouldn't realize

544
00:51:06,440 --> 00:51:11,103
[SPEAKER_04]: Why that sutta talks about paticca-sampāda at the end.

545
00:51:12,984 --> 00:51:19,968
[SPEAKER_04]: And in fact, I've seen people who talk about paticca-sampāda as precisely justifying the view that you create your suffering.

546
00:51:20,868 --> 00:51:25,431
[SPEAKER_04]: That it's like the kamma that you create in the moment through your attention,

547
00:51:26,211 --> 00:51:37,819
[SPEAKER_04]: And then, Pratisana Upāda is like a cycle, or like a sequence of mental events that follow because of your direct kind of action.

548
00:51:38,359 --> 00:51:41,341
[SPEAKER_04]: And the sutta is literally saying, no, it's not.

549
00:51:41,701 --> 00:51:44,103
[SPEAKER_04]: Then, you're the creator of your suffering.

550
00:51:44,903 --> 00:51:53,369
[SPEAKER_04]: So, eh... and so, what the Buddha says, this is kind of the view or the outlook

551
00:51:54,588 --> 00:52:00,773
[SPEAKER_04]: Through which you transcend all of these four wrong ways of looking at it.

552
00:52:01,114 --> 00:52:19,710
[SPEAKER_04]: Like, if you don't see that, you're still liable to assume that you are directly responsible for it, that you can't get rid of it, or that it's because of somebody... it's something somebody else did to you, that you're suffering, or that it's just happening because I ate this food today, so it's kind of randomly arisen.

553
00:52:20,977 --> 00:52:37,187
[SPEAKER_04]: Unless you see that direct escape, quote-unquote, of the normal method, as the Buddha called it, dependent origination, then that's why you will still be subject to those wrong views.

554
00:52:37,647 --> 00:52:42,390
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I think it's also worth mentioning because, like, kind of reading the Sutta, it's kind of like, I mean,

555
00:52:43,242 --> 00:52:44,843
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, easy to say, oh, yeah, of course, of course.

556
00:52:44,903 --> 00:52:46,945
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, I'm not creating this suffering.

557
00:52:47,025 --> 00:52:47,766
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not random.

558
00:52:47,866 --> 00:52:48,546
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not others.

559
00:52:48,586 --> 00:52:49,107
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not both.

560
00:52:49,767 --> 00:52:56,132
[SPEAKER_00]: But, as you just say, like, as long as someone is still subject to suffering, to that extent, he doesn't see this principle.

561
00:52:56,312 --> 00:52:56,473
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

562
00:52:56,493 --> 00:52:58,474
[SPEAKER_00]: And he falls into one of these categories.

